A||N||5||fastback87||Radiator Fan Problems||||5||
Z||000000||fastback87||11-01-2001||02:05 PM||||What would cause my radiator fan to only operate when my air conditioner is turned on?  If I don't turn the air conditioner on my car will overheat everytime.  It is like there is just something not working.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||11-01-2001||02:20 PM||||Year and engine type would be helpful. On the V6 there is a temp switch for the fan by the thermostat housing, Rodney sells a lower temp switch( stock is something like 220 deg). That switch could very well be your problem.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||isthiswhereiputausername?||11-01-2001||04:41 PM||||Could wire it direct and turn it on when you need it on?   It sure give you a better feeling in heavy traffic  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tigger||11-01-2001||04:51 PM||||Some forum members have wired a manual switch through the relay to do the same thing.  Do a search on "manual fan switch," it's been covered a few times.<P>Sounds like all you need is a new temp switch.  I think if you ground the plug for the sensor it will turn the fan on, that will let you know the wiring to the switch is good.  The fan should come on automatically when the A/C is on.  A manual switch is always good to have as a backup if the temp switch in the block stops working.||||reg||1
Z||000004||gomobile||11-01-2001||06:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Tigger:<BR><B>Some forum members have wired a manual switch through the relay to do the same thing.  Do a search on "manual fan switch," it's been covered a few times.<P>Sounds like all you need is a new temp switch.  I think if you ground the plug for the sensor it will turn the fan on, that will let you know the wiring to the switch is good.  The fan should come on automatically when the A/C is on.  A manual switch is always good to have as a backup if the temp switch in the block stops working.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I did one of those manual Fan Switch Istalls and I would say I feel better cause now I can control the fan especially in Southern CAL traffic. No need to buy any other sensor just spent 2-3 bucks for a good switch and wiring (do a search in the forum for the splicing and such - that is if u don't know what to splice).  <BR>||||reg||6
Z||000005||grinthock||11-01-2001||07:10 PM||||I just asked this last week<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/014663.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/014663.html</A> <P>It's probably the sender on the engine, I had to replace mine awhile back, I also did this, I added a manual enable, there is a wire about the brake pedal you just splice it, when it's GROUNDED the fan pop's on, I like it when the traffic slows I can turn it on, the stock one comes on a TAD late for my liking.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/014663.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/014663.html</A> <P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fieroworld.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieroworld.com</A>  The place to go for Fiero INFO! <BR>Home of my 87 GT and 85 GT<BR>Justin C.<BR>Engineer, Designer and<BR>Technical Trainer for Nortel Networks Business Products.||||reg||1
